                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f7cb72b5-e30a-3219-9298-f99868cafb6a",
                        "entry_id": "f62180b1-ae08-34a6-abca-4f8b819bd5f3",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Person, die ein Boot rudert"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "dd013090-ccdf-4302-9384-cf06d37b95e6",
                                "sense_id": "f7cb72b5-e30a-3219-9298-f99868cafb6a",
                                "text": "„Auf die gleiche Weise werden die Sitzbalken für die Ruderer eingezogen und fixiert.“"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5f7ffacd-d77c-4888-8d1c-ed30d5b48c1d",
                                "sense_id": "f7cb72b5-e30a-3219-9298-f99868cafb6a",
                                "text": "„Die Ruderer schafften stromabwärts vier Meilen pro Stunde und stromaufwärts die Hälfte.“"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d3a182af-baee-4221-b5e7-2b736a2ef4ce",
                                "sense_id": "f7cb72b5-e30a-3219-9298-f99868cafb6a",
                                "text": "„Ich verließ Korfu bei Einbruch der Nacht und kam am frühen Morgen in Otranto an, ohne daß meine Ruderer auch nur einmal ihre Ruder eintauchten.“"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7d2ed0e2-b499-46b0-97f0-8f8683174505",
                                "sense_id": "f7cb72b5-e30a-3219-9298-f99868cafb6a",
                                "text": "„Sein Offizier wandte sich nun zu den Ruderern, die noch im Boote saßen, und erteilte ihnen die ihm zugekommenen Orders.“"
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "faf8b59e-949f-483e-85b2-b059309fbbe3",
                        "source_entry_id": "f62180b1-ae08-34a6-abca-4f8b819bd5f3",
                        "source_sense_id": "f7cb72b5-e30a-3219-9298-f99868cafb6a",
                        "source_sense_description": "Person, die ein Boot rudert",
                        "target_lang_word": "oar",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "oar",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "62b6c481-47b2-3d27-8a21-37213045b367",
                                        "entry_id": "a0913bdd-a343-3874-824b-6cbec2b90d51",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A type of lever used to propel a boat, having a flat blade at one end and a handle at the other, and pivoted in a rowlock atop the gunwale, whereby a rower seated in the boat and pulling the handle can pass the blade through the water by repeated strokes against the water's resistance, thus moving the boat."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2c975ad1-8416-4cd6-aa5c-8b759fb821ce",
                                                "sense_id": "62b6c481-47b2-3d27-8a21-37213045b367",
                                                "text": "The oar snaps in his hand\nBefore he reaches dry land\nBut the sound doesn't deafen his smile\nJust pokes at wet sand\nWith an oar in his hand\nFloats off down the river Nile\nFloats off down the river Nile..."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3cbf530a-97cf-3822-8f5c-bd1d9cf83ebd",
                                        "entry_id": "a0913bdd-a343-3874-824b-6cbec2b90d51",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An oarsman; a rower."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "74cc6f58-4572-4447-9c0b-824bed9b77af",
                                                "sense_id": "3cbf530a-97cf-3822-8f5c-bd1d9cf83ebd",
                                                "text": "He is a good oar."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "45c94446-ebb6-32e9-8ba5-06551cc42fdd",
                                        "entry_id": "a0913bdd-a343-3874-824b-6cbec2b90d51",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An oar-like swimming organ of various invertebrates."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "oar",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "678b38ef-18e5-36b0-ba38-f894a3800e6a",
                                        "entry_id": "8786461a-097f-36c3-8e92-ce28917512f9",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To row; to travel with, or as if with, oars."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "literary"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "415a1fbb-1a56-4851-ad1d-603be825102a",
                                                "sense_id": "678b38ef-18e5-36b0-ba38-f894a3800e6a",
                                                "text": "The weather was fine, and whilst oaring along I would fain have landed on the islands between; but fearful of a change, and already half worn-out by my previous trail, I let them go by with the comforting resolve of turning them up on some future occasion."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "69f3f091-1474-41b2-bd63-12991daac214",
                                                "sense_id": "678b38ef-18e5-36b0-ba38-f894a3800e6a",
                                                "text": "Turning the long tables upside down — and there were twelve of them — they seated themselves, one behind another, within the upturned table tops as though they were boats and were about to oar their way into some fabulous ocean."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bea99c63-9d91-4fa1-8ab0-dc202c22b9b3",
                                                "sense_id": "678b38ef-18e5-36b0-ba38-f894a3800e6a",
                                                "text": "In Nopsca's theory, flight evolved as a means of running more quickly over the ground: \"Birds originated from bipedal, long-tailed cursorial reptiles which during running oared along in the air by flapping their free anterior extremities.\""
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "af5e888f-e294-4714-974c-5e6d8900fdcb",
                        "source_entry_id": "f62180b1-ae08-34a6-abca-4f8b819bd5f3",
                        "source_sense_id": "f7cb72b5-e30a-3219-9298-f99868cafb6a",
